Your role is to create a crawler for a website that may publish classical music concerts.

What should the crawler do:

- locate all scrapeable concerts on the website, including past concerts when
  the site still publishes them
- create a universal script that scrapes the concert details

Before implementing, inspect `crawlers/base.py` and at least one current
`crawlers/<country_code>/<site>/main.py` example. Use the repository's current
interfaces exactly; do not rely on remembered crawler APIs. The assigned country
is a hint, not a constraint: if the source is clearly based in a different
country, use that ISO code in `CrawlerConfig`. Use `country_code=None` only for a
genuinely multi-country source and then emit an ISO country code on every record.
A home-country organization does not become multi-country merely because it tours.
If you return `BLOCKED.md`, apply the same resolved geography to its metadata.

To investigate the website, use the Playwright MCP. First, investigate if you can reconstruct some API from the network requests - that would be the easiest, since it usually has the structured data. If you can't find it in network requests, go for HTML parsing.

Concert details should contain following values:
title, date, url, time_from, venue, city, country_code, description, source_url, source

The title, date, URL, venue, city, country code, source URL, and source must never
be empty. Dates must be real calendar dates in YYYY-MM-DD form. Start and end
times and descriptions may be None when the website does not provide them.

Use a reasonable city or venue default when the source provides strong evidence
for one. For example, an institution that publishes events only in its home city
can use that city as a default, and a venue-specific calendar can use that venue.
Do not apply a philharmonic's home city or hall to an event explicitly shown as a
touring performance. Never put prices, ticket text, performers, addresses, or
descriptive prose into city or venue. Never use the city itself as a placeholder
venue. If an individual event's date, city, or venue cannot be extracted or
defensibly inferred, skip that event instead of returning an invalid record.

Decide whether the source is classical-only or broad. A classical-only source
may use the default `upload_target='classical'`. A mixed theatre, festival,
ticketing, or general cultural source must set `CrawlerConfig.upload_target` to
`potential` so the Codex potential-event classifier reviews the events before
they reach `classical_concert`.
Do not upload every event from a mixed source directly as classical music.

What I mean by description: description is a long body of text related to the concert. It can be large and unstructured, and it's important that it includes details about which composers and works are being played (if this is on the website, of course). The reasoning behind this is that the description will be later parsed by AI to extract composers and works.

Save the new crawler at exactly `{{ crawler_directory }}/main.py`. Do not derive
a different directory from redirects or alternate domains.

`CrawlerConfig` supports the fields described below. Start with this minimal
valid configuration and add optional transformation fields when needed:

```python
config = CrawlerConfig(
    slug="site_domain",
    source="Human-readable source name",
    source_url="https://example.com/",
    country_code={{ country_code_literal }},
    upload_target="classical",
)
```

This example is intentionally minimal. It assumes each dictionary returned by
`scrape()` already contains the required `source` and `source_url` record
fields. If those values are constant for every record, prefer adding them with
`front_fields=[('source_url', SOURCE_URL), ('source', SOURCE)]` instead. The
configured `source` and `source_url` values are crawler metadata and are not
automatically copied into scraped records.

Set `slug` to the final directory component of `{{ crawler_directory }}`, and
set `source_url` to the canonical source URL. The required constructor fields
are `slug`, `source`, and `source_url`. The supported optional fields are
`country_code`, `columns`, `upload_target`, `dedupe_subset`, `front_fields`, and
`csv_path`. Do not invent or use unsupported aliases such as `name` or
`csv_filename`. Use `upload_target="potential"` for a broad source as described
above; otherwise retain `"classical"`.

After building, first run a cheap import and instantiation check with system
`python`, not `uv`, to catch syntax errors and an invalid `CrawlerConfig`
constructor. Then run targeted parser checks by calling `scrape()` directly and
inspect representative output records, including their date/city/venue pairs
and duplicates. Do not invoke the module's production `main()` during testing.
The outer worker performs the authoritative full scrape, so you do not need to
repeat a slow full-catalogue run solely for final validation.

Only modify the expected new crawler directory. Do not modify shared crawler
code, dependencies, workflows, prompts, configuration, environment files, or
other crawlers. Store downloaded HTML, JSON responses, screenshots, and every
other investigation artifact under `/tmp`, never inside the repository. Before
finishing, run `git status --short` and remove every change outside the assigned
crawler directory. Do not commit or push changes.

Use the repository's structured logger for operational output:
`from observability import log_message`. Do not call `print()` from a production
crawler; generated crawler PR validation rejects it. Prefer structured fields
such as `url`, `record_count`, `error_type`, and `error_message`, and never log
raw HTML, full event descriptions, credentials, or tokens.

Implement the crawler as a `BaseCrawler` subclass with a `scrape()` method.
During investigation and testing call `scrape()` directly. Do not call
`BaseCrawler.run()`, upload data, connect to the database, or write a CSV.
This restriction applies only to investigation and testing. The committed
module is a production crawler: it must define a top-level `main()` function
that calls the crawler instance's `run()` method so the normal scheduler saves
the CSV and uploads the records. Also call `main()` from an
`if __name__ == '__main__':` guard. Do not use a scrape-only or print-only
implementation of `main()`.
If retrying a directory that already contains `BLOCKED.md`, delete
`BLOCKED.md` when a working `main.py` is successfully created. Never leave
both files in the directory.

If it is not possible to build a working crawler after investigating API and
HTML options, create `{{ crawler_directory }}/BLOCKED.md` but do not create
`main.py`. Explain:

- the original URL
- why a crawler cannot currently be implemented
- which API, network, and HTML approaches were attempted
- what would unblock implementation

Also use `BLOCKED.md` when the source exposes no scrapeable concerts, including
in its available archives. Start `BLOCKED.md` with exactly this metadata block,
using one of `no_current_events`, `access_blocked`, `no_parseable_source`, or
`implementation_failed` as the reason code. The outer worker will normalize
